### Which @angular/* package(s) are the source of the bug?

forms

### Is this a regression?

No

### Description

When a `SignalFormControl` is placed inside a regular `FormGroup`, rule-driven status changes are not propagated to the parent. The control itself reports the correct status, but the parent `FormGroup` keeps its previous status and value, and emits nothing.

This affects the pattern shown in the migration guide (https://angular.dev/guide/forms/signals/migration#disabling-enabling-control), where an external signal drives `disabled()` / `required()` / `applyWhen()`:

- `required(p, { when: () => isRequired() })` switched off on an empty value: control -> `VALID`, parent `FormGroup` stays `INVALID`.
- `disabled(p, { when: () => isDisabled() })` switched on: control -> `DISABLED`, parent `FormGroup` keeps the control in `value` and keeps the previous status (with a plain `FormControl` the disabled control would be excluded from the parent value and validity).

In both cases `parent.valueChanges` and `parent.statusChanges` emit 0 times. The parent catches up only on the next value change of the control.

### Expected behavior

The parent `FormGroup` recomputes its value and validity whenever the `SignalFormControl` status changes, as it does for a `FormControl` (status/value of the parent consistent with the child; disabled child excluded from the parent value and validity).

### Actual behavior

The parent is only notified when the control value changes.

### Where in the source

`packages/forms/signals/compat/src/signal_form_control/signal_form_control.ts` (main):

- the value effect (L134-L144) calls `notifyParentUnlessPending()` -> `parent.updateValueAndValidity()`;
- the status effect (L147-L156) only emits the control's own `statusChanges` / `StatusChangeEvent` and does not notify the parent.

`packages/forms/signals/test/node/compat/signal_form_control_in_form_group.spec.ts` only covers validity changes caused by a value change; there is no test where a rule driven by an external signal changes the status.

Possible fix: notify the parent from the status effect as well (inside `untracked`), respecting `pendingParentNotifications` to avoid double updates.

### Workaround

```ts
effect(() => {
  void control.status; // tracks the Signal Forms signals read by the getter
  untracked(() => control.parent?.updateValueAndValidity());
});
```

### Minimal reproduction

No StackBlitz, self-contained spec:

```ts
import { signal } from '@angular/core';
import { TestBed } from '@angular/core/testing';
import { FormControl, FormGroup } from '@angular/forms';
import { disabled, required } from '@angular/forms/signals';
import { SignalFormControl } from '@angular/forms/signals/compat';

it('parent FormGroup is not updated on rule-driven status changes', () => {
  const isRequired = signal(true);
  const isDisabled = signal(false);
  const name = TestBed.runInInjectionContext(
    () =>
      new SignalFormControl('', (p) => {
        required(p, { when: () => isRequired() });
        disabled(p, { when: () => isDisabled() });
      }),
  );
  const group = new FormGroup({ name, age: new FormControl(25) });
  TestBed.tick(); // flush initial effect runs, as on first render

  let emissions = 0;
  group.statusChanges.subscribe(() => emissions++);

  isRequired.set(false);
  TestBed.tick();
  expect(name.status).toBe('VALID');
  expect(group.status).toBe('VALID'); // FAILS: 'INVALID'

  isDisabled.set(true);
  TestBed.tick();
  expect(name.status).toBe('DISABLED');
  expect(group.value).toEqual({ age: 25 }); // FAILS: { name: '', age: 25 }

  expect(emissions).toBeGreaterThan(0); // FAILS: 0
});
```

Note: without the first `TestBed.tick()` the test may pass by accident, because the initial run of the value effect notifies the parent after the rule has already changed.

### Exception or error

No exception. Failing expectations:

```
expect(group.status).toBe('VALID')        -> received 'INVALID'
expect(group.value).toEqual({ age: 25 })  -> received { name: '', age: 25 }
expect(emissions).toBeGreaterThan(0)      -> received 0
```

### Environment

```
@angular/core 22.2.2
@angular/forms 22.2.2
Node 24.21.0
```

Also reproduced on @angular/forms 22.2.6.
Same code on angular/angular main (checked 2026-11-12).

**Which @angular/* package(s) are the source of the bug?** core

**Is this a regression?** No

**Description**

With `withIncrementalHydration()` and `@defer (hydrate on viewport)`, a click or keydown on an element inside the block is lost when it arrives after the viewport trigger started hydrating the block but before hydration finished.

`hydrateAndInvokeBlockListeners` (`packages/core/src/hydration/event_replay.ts`) queues the event and calls `triggerHydrationFromBlockName(injector, blockName, replayFn)`. Because the viewport trigger already put the block in `dehydratedBlockRegistry.hydrating`, `triggerHydrationFromBlockName` (`packages/core/src/defer/triggering.ts`) returns at:

```ts
if (blocksBeingHydrated.has(blockName)) {
  return;
}
```

The in-flight hydration was started by the viewport trigger with no `replayQueuedEventsFn`, so nothing replays the queue when it finishes. The event stays in `EVENT_REPLAY_QUEUE` until some later event-triggered hydration happens to flush it, or forever.

Present in 22.2.6 and on `main`.

**Reproduction** (seen in a production app; these steps are the reduced shape, not yet a standalone StackBlitz)

1. SSR app with `provideClientHydration(withIncrementalHydration())`.
2. `@defer (hydrate on viewport) { <button (click)="count = count + 1">{{ count }}</button> }` below the fold, with a lazily loaded dependency so hydration takes a few hundred ms (throttle the network).
3. Scroll the block into view and click the button immediately, before hydration finishes.

Expected: the click is replayed after hydration and `count` becomes 1. Actual: `count` stays 0.

**Proposed fix**

When the block is already hydrating, replay the queued events once that hydration settles:

```ts
const hydrating = blocksBeingHydrated.get(blockName);
if (hydrating) {
  if (replayQueuedEventsFn) {
    hydrating.promise.then(() => replayQueuedEventsFn([blockName]), () => {});
  }
  return;
}
```

`createReplayQueuedBlockEventsFn` already requeues events for blocks not in the list, so passing `[blockName]` replays only this block's events.

### Which @angular/* package(s) are the source of the bug?

core

### Is this a regression?

No

### Description

A template-only hot update can replace a view child while leaving a one-time binding in its parent pointed at the detached child. I have not identified a previously working HMR version, so I marked regression as No.

### Minimal reproduction without Angular Material

In a standalone Angular CLI app, use this root component and external template:

```ts
import { Component, ElementRef, ViewChild } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  templateUrl: './app.component.html',
})
export class AppComponent {
  @ViewChild('target') target!: ElementRef<HTMLElement>;
  private original?: HTMLElement;
  result = '';

  ngAfterViewInit() {
    this.original = this.target.nativeElement;
  }

  compare() {
    const current = this.target.nativeElement;
    this.result = 'Same=' + (this.original === current) +
      ' OriginalConnected=' + this.original?.isConnected +
      ' CurrentConnected=' + current.isConnected;
  }
}
```

```html
<h1 #target>Before</h1>
<button (click)="compare()">Compare</button>
<p>{{ result }}</p>
```

1. Run `ng serve --hmr`, open the app, and click Compare. All three values are true.
2. Change only `Before` to `After` in the HTML and save. Wait for the heading to change without manually reloading.
3. Click Compare again. The result is `Same=false OriginalConnected=false CurrentConnected=true`.

### User-visible impact

In a Material table with 20 rows and five rows per page, assign `dataSource.paginator = this.paginator` in `ngAfterViewInit()`, with `paginator` queried using `@ViewChild(MatPaginator)`. Changing only a header label from “Status” to “State” under HMR leaves five rows visible while the paginator shows `0 of 0`. Next page is disabled, and choosing a page size of 20 still displays five rows.

I compared the same HTML edit with `--hmr` and `--no-hmr` on Angular 22.2.6. With HMR disabled, the automatic full reload leaves the paginator working, including navigation to rows 6–10. A manual reload also restores pagination. Rebinding from a `@ViewChild` setter worked around the table symptom with HMR enabled.

### Expected behavior / clarification

I would expect the text-only edit to leave a previously working control usable, or to fall back to a full reload when the binding cannot be preserved. Is this a supported limitation of template HMR? If so, could the HMR documentation describe the impact on one-time view-child bindings?

